.TH DS 1 "August 2026" "ds 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ds \- domain search: check domain availability across many TLDs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ds
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR NAME .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ds
checks whether domain names are registered. Each name is checked against every
TLD selected with
.BR \-\-tld ,
in parallel.
.PP
A lookup asks RDAP first, using the server list from the IANA bootstrap file
(cached locally for seven days). TLDs with no RDAP service fall back to WHOIS
over port 43, using the server and "available" needle table in
.IR whois.json ,
which is embedded in the binary at compile time. If a bundled WHOIS host is
stale or missing, IANA is asked which server serves that TLD today.
.PP
A lookup that cannot be answered is reported as
.B UNKNOWN
with the reason attached, never guessed as available.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IR NAME .\|.\|.
Names to check. A name may be a bare label
.RB ( apple ),
a comma separated list
.RB ( apple,orange,bangla ),
or
.BI @ file
to read one name per line (commas and
.B #
comments allowed). Several arguments may be given. A name containing a dot is
treated as a whole domain and checked as given when
.B \-\-tld
is omitted.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Selecting TLDs
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tld " " \fILIST\fR
Comma separated TLDs, or one of
.BR all " (every known TLD),"
.BR popular " (a curated set),"
.B rdap
(only TLDs with an RDAP service), or
.BI @ file
for a list in a file. Without this option a bare label is checked as
.IR NAME .com.
.TP
.BR \-\-level " " \fIany\fR|\fIsecond\fR|\fIthird\fR
Which level the name would sit at.
.B second
keeps plain TLDs
.RI ( apple.com ),
.B third
keeps multi-label suffixes
.RI ( apple.co.uk ).
Filters the
.B \-\-tld
list only; a whole domain typed out by hand is always checked.
.TP
.B \-\-cctld
Only country-code TLDs, the two-letter ones. Shorthand for
.BR "\-\-tld\-len 2" .
.TP
.BR \-\-tld\-len " " \fIN\fR|\fIMIN\-MAX\fR
Keep TLDs of this length:
.BR 2 ", " 2\-3 ", " \-3 " (at most three), " 4\- " (at least four)."
Measured on the last label, so
.I co.uk
counts as two.
.SS Showing more
.TP
.B \-\-details
Registrar, IANA ID, creation, update and expiry dates, status codes,
nameservers, DNSSEC and abuse contact.
.TP
.B \-\-registry
Which RDAP endpoint and WHOIS server answered.
.TP
.B \-\-whois
Query WHOIS as well and print the raw record.
.TP
.B \-\-dns\-records
Look up A, AAAA, NS, MX, TXT, CNAME and SOA records.
.TP
.B \-\-where
For available domains, print the registry operator, its official registration
page (both from IANA's record for the TLD) and registrar searches with the name
filled in.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Include the raw RDAP JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-info
Shorthand for
.BR \-\-details " " \-\-registry " " \-\-whois " " \-\-dns\-records " " \-\-where .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a JSON array instead of the text report.
.TP
.B \-\-available\-only
Print only available domains. Saved files still cover every result.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Only print the summary.
.SS Saving results
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-save
Write
.IR available.txt ,
.I unavailable.txt
and, when a registry could not be reached,
.IR unknown.txt .
Nothing is written without this.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory for those files. Implies
.BR \-\-save .
.TP
.B \-\-append
Append to the files instead of overwriting them. Implies
.BR \-\-save .
.SS Where to look
.TP
.BR \-\-source " " \fIauto\fR|\fIrdap\fR|\fIwhois\fR
.B auto
tries RDAP, then the bundled table, then an IANA referral.
.B rdap
uses RDAP alone.
.B whois
uses the bundled
.I whois.json
alone.
.TP
.BR \-\-rdap\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Custom RDAP server list, in the RDAP bootstrap format IANA publishes (RFC
9224): each entry maps a list of TLDs to a list of servers, tried in order.
Without this option
.I ./rdap.json
and
.I ~/.config/ds/rdap.json
are used when they exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-rdap\-mode " " \fImerge\fR|\fIonly\fR
.B merge
(default) lets the custom entries win for the TLDs they name and takes the rest
from the IANA bootstrap.
.B only
uses the custom list alone and never downloads the bootstrap.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-iana
Never query whois.iana.org: no referral when a bundled WHOIS host is stale, and
no registry names for
.BR \-\-where .
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Re-download the IANA RDAP bootstrap file.
.SS Pacing
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrency " " \fIN\fR
Parallel lookups. Default 20.
.TP
.BR \-\-per\-host " " \fIN\fR
Parallel lookups against a single registry server. Default 4.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fISECS\fR
Per-request timeout. Default 10.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Plain output. Also honoured:
.BR NO_COLOR .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one domain is available.
.TP
.B 1
None are.
.TP
.B 2
A startup error, such as an unusable
.B \-\-tld
list.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/ds/rdap\-dns.json
Cached copy of the IANA RDAP bootstrap, refreshed after seven days or on
.BR \-\-refresh .
Honours
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME .
.TP
.IR ./rdap.json ", " ~/.config/ds/rdap.json
Custom RDAP server lists, used automatically when present. See
.BR \-\-rdap\-file .
.TP
.IR available.txt ", " unavailable.txt ", " unknown.txt
Written in the working directory, or
.BR \-\-out\-dir ,
when
.B \-\-save
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Check one name across a few TLDs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds apple \-\-tld com,net,io
.EE
.RE
.PP
Sweep every TLD where the name is registered at the second level, saving the
results:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds mybrand \-\-tld all \-\-level second \-\-available\-only \-\-save
.EE
.RE
.PP
Several names at once, read from a file, across the country-code TLDs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds @names.txt \-\-tld all \-\-cctld \-\-level second
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything known about one domain:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds apple.com \-\-all\-info
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Large sweeps get rate-limited.
.B ds
paces itself per host, backs off on HTTP 403 and 429, and stops querying a
server that has refused it six times in a row, retrying after thirty seconds.
Identity Digital runs roughly 250 gTLDs behind one RDAP endpoint with a strict
quota, so a single
.B "\-\-tld all"
sweep will leave some of those unresolved; the summary says how many, and they
are worth re-running later.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whois (1),
.BR dig (1)
.PP
RFC 9083 (RDAP responses), RFC 9224 (RDAP bootstrap), RFC 3912 (WHOIS).
